<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;"><b>Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</b></h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Legend</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Summarize a classmate's presentation for a teacher. Communication: Assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can summarize a classmate's presentation for the teacher, using key points (e.g., "He told a story about a hero who was fighting a monster.").<br>Can assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers by using simple gestures (e.g., says, "The hero is brave." and shows a strong pose).<br>Can help clarify for peers the main idea of a legend (e.g., says, "This story is about bravery." and gives a thumbs-up).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"></p>
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;"><b>The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</b></h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of famous Panamanian legend characters (e.g., La Tulivieja, El Chupacabras) on the board. Students are asked to recall the names of the legends and one key detail from each. For example: "Who is this?" (Points to La Tulivieja). "What was she doing?" (Expected answer: "She was looking for her baby."). This activates prior knowledge of legends and uses past continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to summarize a short statement and clarify a misunderstanding. Teacher says: "Listen. Yesterday, Pedro was telling us about La India Dormida. He said she was a beautiful princess who was sleeping on the mountain. What did Pedro say?" Teacher then models: "He said she was a princess sleeping on a mountain." (Summarizing). Teacher then simulates a misunderstanding: "Teacher, was she fighting?" (Student asks). Teacher responds: "No, she wasn't fighting. She was sleeping. (Shows sleeping gesture). This story is about love." (Clarifying with gestures and simple language).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I summarize, do I say every single word?" (Expected answer: "No, just the main ideas.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If my friend doesn't understand, what can I do?" (Expected answer: "Explain it in simpler words or use a gesture.")</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Audio recording of a short (30-45 sec) summary of a well-known Panamanian legend (e.g., El Hombre Caimán). The summary uses past simple and past continuous, and key vocabulary (e.g., <i>river, transform, creature, fish, brave</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Clarify details in group discussions and present short stories or legends to peers (e.g., "The man was fighting the monster when the village was attacked.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b><br>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher plays the audio once. Students answer: "What is the main idea of this legend summary?" (e.g., "It's about a man who became an alligator.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher plays the audio a second time. Students listen and mark True or False for statements like: "The man was swimming when he transformed." (True/False). "The villagers were happy about the creature." (True/False). Focus on details mentioned in the summary, including past continuous and simple actions.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Mediation)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Summarizing main points of a legend using past simple and past continuous. Clarifying information. Vocabulary: <i>legend, monster, hero, village, river, fight, save, transform, brave, mysterious, once upon a time, suddenly, in the end.</i> Pronunciation: practice of -ed and -ing endings (e.g., *narrated, fighting*).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b><br>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Summary Cloze:</b> The teacher provides a short text about a new legend (or a simple version of a known one). Students work individually to complete a cloze summary using given vocabulary words (e.g., *hero, fighting, saved, village, monster*) and past tense verbs. Example: "Long ago, a ___ (hero) was ___ (fighting) a terrible ___ (monster). He ___ (saved) the ___ (village) from danger." Teacher checks answers as a class, focusing on pronunciation of -ed endings.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Sentence Summaries:</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher writes simple sentences from a legend on the board, e.g., "The brave hero was exploring the mountain." Student A reads a sentence. Student B summarizes it for Student A, e.g., "You said the hero was exploring the mountain." Then they swap roles. The teacher encourages using sentence starters for summarizing: "He mentioned...", "She said...", "The main idea is...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Clarifying Legend Points:</b> Students are paired up. Each student has a small card with a single, slightly complex sentence about a legend they discussed in previous lessons (e.g., "The spirit was calling the children while they were playing near the river."). Student A reads their sentence to Student B. Student B tries to summarize it. If Student B struggles, Student A must simplify or clarify using gestures or simpler words (e.g., "The spirit... calling children... river... children playing..."). They then switch cards and roles.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Small group "Comic Strip Showcase and Mediation". Students are in groups of 3-4. Each student presents their "Comic Strip Panamanian Legend" project to their group. One student presents, and another student in the group is designated as the "mediator" for a round. The teacher walks around, listening to presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b><br>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in their groups with their comic strips ready.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A presents their comic strip, narrating the legend using past simple and past continuous (e.g., "Once upon a time, the hero was exploring the jungle when suddenly he saw a hidden treasure."). They describe the key characters (<i>hero, monster, witch</i>) and events (<i>fight, save, rescue, disappear, transform</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After Student A finishes, the teacher or another group member (acting as the teacher's "assistant") asks a designated mediator from the group to summarize Student A's presentation for the "teacher" (or the rest of the group). Example prompt: "Could you summarize Maria's legend for me, [Mediator Name]?" The mediator uses key points and target language (e.g., "She told a story about a brave hero who was fighting a mysterious monster.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">If there is a misunderstanding or a peer needs clarification on a point (e.g., "Was the witch good or evil?"), the mediator assists in clarifying, possibly using gestures or simpler words: "The witch was evil (shows thumbs down). This story is about defeating evil."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Groups rotate roles until everyone has had a chance to present, summarize, and clarify. The teacher observes and takes notes.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on mediation skills, use of target language, and pronunciation during the "Comic Strip Showcase."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their comic strip and actively participate in peer mediation, either by summarizing a classmate's presentation for the teacher or by clarifying a point for a peer.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Summary:</b> Can summarize a classmate's presentation using key points. (e.g., "He told a story about a hero who was fighting a monster.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Assistance in Clarification:</b> Can assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers using simple gestures and clear language. (e.g., says, "The hero is brave." and shows a strong pose).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Appropriate use of past simple (e.g., *saved, celebrated*) and past continuous (e.g., *was fighting, was exploring*) to narrate and summarize.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Incorporates relevant vocabulary (<i>legend, monster, hero, village, fight, save, brave, mysterious, once upon a time, suddenly</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation, especially of -ed and -ing endings.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:<br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned today about summarizing a legend is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When my classmate didn't understand, I helped by..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of summarizing a legend for the teacher was easiest for me? What was most challenging?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I better help my friends understand difficult parts of a story in English next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b><br>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in promoting mediation skills today? Were students able to summarize clearly and assist peers effectively?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students consistently use past simple and past continuous in their comic strip narrations and summaries? Was the pronunciation of -ed and -ing endings accurate? What grammatical structures or vocabulary words require further practice?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed to improve students' ability to mediate information, especially for those who struggled with clarifying misunderstandings?</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the unit on "My Favorite Legend" and the 21st Century Skills Project. The mediation skills practiced today are foundational for future lessons involving collaborative projects and understanding complex texts in English.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>